// GPU-side pool allocation for the brick volume (docs/gpu-streaming-architecture.md P1).
//
// WHAT THIS FILE KILLS, IN ONE LINE. The per-chunk totals readback: today a chunk's
// occupancy/material dword counts cross PCIe so FVoxelBrickPool::AllocateForChunk can
// hand out arena ranges on the CPU, and that round trip is the fence the whole GPU
// arm queues behind (dispatchToReady 28-37 ms of which ~28 ms is GPU work -- the rest
// is the trip; queued p50 1,602 ms behind it). With these kernels the generation
// graph CLAIMS its own ranges with atomics, writes its words, descriptors and record
// in the same graph, and reads back NOTHING.
//
// ONE ALLOCATOR, NOT TWO -- the owner's decision (2026-08-23), and the design's
// central statement. When voxel.GPU.PoolAlloc is armed, THIS allocator owns the
// entire occupancy and material arenas (RegionFirst 0, RegionWords the full
// capacity), and BOTH producers claim from it:
//
//   * THE GPU PRODUCER (pure worldgen, the steady state) claims in its own
//     generation graph: the claim pass reads the scan's totals where they were
//     produced and nothing crosses PCIe.
//   * THE CPU PRODUCER (edited chunks, cold fallback, and the bit-exact oracle
//     voxel.GPU.VerifyCoarse depends on -- it stays a producer permanently) knows
//     its sizes up front, so its pack is UPLOADED to transient scratch and the
//     SAME claim + write kernels run over it in the pool's flush command, with
//     the totals arriving as a 2-dword upload instead of a scan output. Slower
//     and coarser than the GPU path, deliberately -- it is off the steady-state
//     hot loop -- but it draws from the SAME free space through the SAME atomics,
//     so the two producers can never be handed the same words.
//
//   DESCRIPTOR SLOTS AND THE CHUNK RECORD SLOT stay CPU-allocated for both
//   producers. That is not a second allocator over the same resource: slots are
//   FIXED SIZE (64 descriptors, one 64 B record, always -- collapsed bricks
//   included), need no generated sizes, and their identity must be CPU-visible
//   for eviction, the march-index delta and FindChunkSlot. Each resource has
//   exactly one dispenser: slots the CPU's DescArena, words this buffer.
//
// THE ALLOCATOR: SIZE-CLASS BUMP + FREE STACKS, chosen over a first-fit free list
// on the GPU for one reason -- a concurrent first-fit scan with coalescing is a
// correctness project of its own, and a wrong allocation here does not crash, it
// writes one chunk's bricks over another's and renders as plausible terrain. So:
//
//   * A request is ROUNDED UP to a size class (occ: steps of OccClassStep dwords,
//     mat: MatClassStep). Class-sized ranges are interchangeable within a class.
//   * ALLOCATE: pop the class's free stack (LIFO, one InterlockedAdd on its top);
//     on empty, bump the region cursor by the class size (one InterlockedAdd).
//   * FREE: push the range back onto its class stack. No coalescing needed, ever,
//     because a freed range is exactly the size the next same-class claim wants.
//
// FRAGMENTATION, STATED HONESTLY. External fragmentation is ZERO by construction --
// classes never split or merge ranges. The costs are (a) INTERNAL padding, the
// class round-up, counted exactly (PaddedCum vs ActualCum counters; the original
// ~5-15% estimate at 128/512-dword steps MEASURED at 39.7-40.8% on the first
// honest legs, 2026-08-23 -- the mat arena's 512-dword step against a ~208-dword
// mean claim is the dominant term, and the claim kernel's demand histogram now
// exists to choose the replacement steps from evidence) and (b) STRANDING: dwords parked
// in the free stack of a class the current mix no longer asks for. Both are printed
// on the [brick-gpualloc] window line (padding %, per-arena stranded MiB, bump
// high-water), so a caller finds out from the log, not from an allocFail. When the
// region genuinely runs out (bump exhausted AND stack empty) the claim FAILS: the
// chunk's record is written as zeros ("nothing here"), the failure is counted by
// reason, and the world shows a missing chunk -- never a corrupted one.
//
// THE GATE THAT CAN FAIL: THE PAGE BITMAP. Every class step is one PAGE, and the
// claim sets its pages with InterlockedOr, checking the PREVIOUS bits. A page that
// was already set means the allocator granted the same dwords twice -- the exact
// silent-corruption failure this codebase fears most -- and it is caught AT THE
// MOMENT OF ALLOCATION, always-on, counted into BitmapCollisions (the claim then
// fails rather than writing over the other tenant). The free pass symmetrically
// checks the bits it clears WERE set (FreeBitmapMissing counts a range freed twice
// or never granted). Both counters are read back asynchronously once per stats
// window and any non-zero value logs as a cross-check FAIL. BrickPoolAllocVerifyMain
// below adds the sampled record-vs-CPU-expectation check on top.
//
// LAYOUT CONTRACT. Every offset, class step, stack capacity and record stride below
// is BOUND FROM THE HOST (FVoxelBrickPool::GetGpuAllocLayout), never a literal --
// the ChunkRecordDwords rule. The state buffer is one RWStructuredBuffer<uint>:
//
//   [0]                 occ bump cursor, REGION-RELATIVE dwords (also the high-water)
//   [1]                 mat bump cursor
//   [2..31]             counters -- see the kCtr* indices, mirrored in
//                       FVoxelBrickPool (kGpuAllocCtr*); indices, not meanings, are
//                       what the two sides must agree on
//   [OccHistFirst..]    claim-size demand histogram, occ (OccHistBuckets entries
//                       of OccHistBucketWords dwords each) -- see the claim kernel
//   [MatHistFirst..]    claim-size demand histogram, mat
//   [OccTopsFirst..]    occ class stack tops (OccClasses entries)
//   [MatTopsFirst..]    mat class stack tops (MatClasses entries)
//   [OccStackFirst..]   occ stack storage, FreeStackCap entries per class
//   [MatStackFirst..]   mat stack storage
//
// The bitmap is its own buffer ([OccBitmapFirst..] then [MatBitmapFirst..], one bit
// per REGION-RELATIVE page). The side table is 4 dwords per chunk slot:
// {occBaseAbs, occClass, matBaseAbs, matClass} -- it exists because FREEING without
// a readback needs the ranges to live somewhere the FREE PASS can read: the CPU
// knows WHICH slot to evict but never learned the ranges (that knowledge is what
// the readback used to carry), so the eviction pass looks them up here. Class 0
// means "no allocation in this arena" (an all-collapsed chunk).
//
// CONCURRENCY NOTE, RECORDED BECAUSE IT IS AN ASSUMPTION AND NOT A PROOF. Claims
// run in generation graphs; frees run in the pool's flush command. RDG places
// UAV barriers between passes that share these buffers, so a push and a pop are
// never live at the same instant and the classic stack race (pop reading a slot
// its pusher has not written) cannot occur under today's scheduling. If that ever
// changes, the corruption it causes is a double-grant -- which is exactly what the
// page bitmap exists to catch. The gate covers the assumption.

#include "/Engine/Public/Platform.ush"

// --- resources ---------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Loose globals, Unreal's $Globals convention, bound by reflected name from the
// FParameters structs in VoxelGpuWorldGen.cpp -- VoxelBrickPoolWrite.usf's model.

// The allocator state (bumps, counters, stack tops, stack storage).
RWStructuredBuffer<uint> AllocState;
// One bit per region-relative page, both arenas.
RWStructuredBuffer<uint> AllocBitmap;
// 4 dwords per chunk slot: occBaseAbs, occClass, matBaseAbs, matClass.
RWStructuredBuffer<uint> AllocSide;
// The per-job claim result: [0] ok, [1] occBaseAbs, [2] occWords (ACTUAL, not
// padded), [3] matBaseAbs, [4] matWords, [5] fail reason, [6..7] spare.
RWStructuredBuffer<uint> OutClaim;
StructuredBuffer<uint>   InClaim;

// The producing graph's outputs, exactly as VoxelBrickPoolWrite.usf names them.
StructuredBuffer<uint>  InBrickTotals;  // [0] occ dwords, [1] mat dwords
// P3 worklist ClassifyTotals stage: base ELEMENT added to the CLASSIC-FORM
// totals reads ([0]/[1]) when InBrickTotals is the worklist totals arena
// (sliceIndex * 2). 0 on every classic dispatch -- a uniform +0. The
// stack-claim form (TotalsChunkIndexPlusOne != 0) never reads through it:
// fused stacks take no worklist feed.
uint TotalsReadBase;
// P3 worklist Pack stage: element bases added into OutClaim[6]/[7] so the
// word-copy kernels source from the worklist pack arenas. 0 classic.
uint SrcWordsOccBase;
uint SrcWordsMatBase;
StructuredBuffer<uint>  InWords;        // scratch words, one arena per pass
StructuredBuffer<uint2> InBrickDesc;    // scratch descriptors, chunk-relative
StructuredBuffer<uint>  InBrickOcc;     // scratch occupancy, for allSolid
StructuredBuffer<uint>  InChunkBrickMask;

// The pool arenas.
RWStructuredBuffer<uint>  OutWords;
RWStructuredBuffer<uint2> OutBrickDesc;
RWStructuredBuffer<uint>  OutChunkTable;

// Free pass: the slots to retire. Verify pass: the expectation table.
StructuredBuffer<uint> InSlotList;
StructuredBuffer<uint> InExpect;
// Verify read-only views (a kernel must never bind one buffer as UAV and SRV).
StructuredBuffer<uint> InPoolTable;
StructuredBuffer<uint> InAllocSide;
StructuredBuffer<uint> InAllocBitmap;
// [0] mismatched dwords, [1] chunks verified, [2] unwritten records seen.
RWStructuredBuffer<uint> OutVerify;

// --- parameters --------------------------------------------------------------
// The region partition and layout, all bound from FVoxelBrickPool::GetGpuAllocLayout.
uint OccRegionFirst;   // ABSOLUTE first dword of the GPU occ region in the arena
uint OccRegionWords;
uint MatRegionFirst;
uint MatRegionWords;
uint OccClassStep;     // dwords per occ class (and per occ bitmap page)
uint OccClasses;
uint MatClassStep;
uint MatClasses;
uint FreeStackCap;     // entries per class stack
uint OccTopsFirst;     // state-buffer dword offsets
uint MatTopsFirst;
uint OccStackFirst;
uint MatStackFirst;
uint OccBitmapFirst;   // bitmap-buffer dword offsets
uint MatBitmapFirst;
// The claim-size demand histogram's geometry (state-buffer offsets and bucket
// widths/counts), host-bound like every other layout number. Added 2026-08-23
// because the first honest legs read padding 39.7-40.8% against the 5-15% the
// header expected, and the ONLY defensible response to that is to measure the
// claim-size DISTRIBUTION before touching the class steps: the recorded means
// (~194 occ / ~208 mat dwords) sit just above class boundaries in a way that
// makes some "obvious" step changes exact no-ops -- ceil(194/64)*64 = 256 =
// ceil(194/128)*128, so the recommended OccStep 64 sweep would move occ padding
// by ZERO if claims cluster at the mean. This project has a recorded case of
// exactly that shape (a caps sweep that moved throughput 0.0); the histogram is
// what prevents the rerun.
uint OccHistFirst;
uint OccHistBucketWords;
uint OccHistBuckets;
uint MatHistFirst;
uint MatHistBucketWords;
uint MatHistBuckets;

// Per-chunk values, same meanings as VoxelBrickPoolWrite.usf's.
uint ChunkSlot;
uint BrickBase;
uint BrickCount;
uint RingLevel;
uint ChunkRecordDwords;
uint ChunkClimatePacked;
uint ChunkSurfaceGradPacked;
uint ChunkSurfaceZRelBits;
int3 OriginVoxel;
uint OccWorstWords;    // scratch worst case; a total above it is a scan fault
uint MatWorstWords;
uint MaxWords;         // copy pass: worst-case dispatch bound for one arena
uint WordsClaimIndex;  // copy pass: 2 selects occ (claim[1]/[2]), 0 means read
                       // occ pair, 1 means mat pair -- see the copy kernel
uint NumSlots;         // free pass
uint NumEntries;       // verify pass

// --- stack-claim (B.1 fusion under voxel.GPU.PoolAlloc, 2026-08-23) ----------
//
// A fused Z-stack runs ONE region graph for K chunks; BrickStackTotalsMain
// writes [0..1] region totals then [2+2c, 3+2c] per-chunk pairs. Each member's
// claim reads ITS OWN pair and derives its scratch prefix (where its words
// start in the SHARED batch scratch) by summing the pairs before it -- the
// same prefix the CPU harvest used to compute from the totals readback this
// path deletes. 0 = classic single-chunk claim, byte-identical: totals at
// [0..1], prefix 0. Encoded as index+1 so the zero default cannot be confused
// with "member 0" (member 0 is value 1, prefix 0, totals at [2..3]).
uint TotalsChunkIndexPlusOne;
// Stack size K, 0 on the classic path. Non-zero arms the SPLIT GATE inside the
// claim: sum(per-chunk pairs) must equal the region pair -- the cross-check the
// CPU harvest used to run on the totals readback, now run where the totals
// live. A wrong split would copy one chunk's words into another chunk's claimed
// range: correctly-allocated addresses, wrong bytes, plausible terrain -- the
// exact class the bitmap gate CANNOT see, which is why the check must not die
// with the readback.
uint TotalsNumChunks;
// Copy/desc/record passes: where this member's slice starts in the shared
// scratch. 0 on the classic path (whole scratch is the chunk's).
uint SrcDescBase;      // descriptor slice: chunkIndex * bricks-per-chunk
uint ChunkMaskBase;    // L1-mask slot: chunkIndex * 2

// Counter indices in AllocState. MIRRORED in FVoxelBrickPool (kGpuAllocCtr*);
// a drifted index misattributes a counter, it does not corrupt terrain, which is
// why params were not spent on all of them -- but keep the two lists in sync.
static const uint kCtrOccBump          = 0u;
static const uint kCtrMatBump          = 1u;
static const uint kCtrClaims           = 2u;
static const uint kCtrStackPops        = 3u;
static const uint kCtrClaimFailOcc     = 4u;
static const uint kCtrClaimFailMat     = 5u;
static const uint kCtrClaimFailWorst   = 6u;
static const uint kCtrBitmapCollision  = 7u;   // the always-on FAIL
static const uint kCtrFrees            = 8u;
static const uint kCtrFreePushOverflow = 9u;
static const uint kCtrFreeBitmapMissing= 10u;  // the free-side FAIL
static const uint kCtrOccInFlight      = 11u;  // padded dwords currently live
static const uint kCtrMatInFlight      = 12u;
static const uint kCtrOccPaddedCum     = 13u;
static const uint kCtrOccActualCum     = 14u;
static const uint kCtrMatPaddedCum     = 15u;
static const uint kCtrMatActualCum     = 16u;
// High-water of any single class's free-stack DEPTH, per arena -- the counter
// that sizes FreeStackCap from evidence instead of hope. Recorded as the depth
// a push WANTED (Old + 1, before the overflow check), so a burst deeper than
// the stack still reports its true demand: a peak above FreeStackCap is the
// FAILING reading and says exactly how far under-provisioned the stack was --
// it is what 16,736 / 1,164 / 29,012 leakedRuns across three identical-code
// legs (2026-08-23) never told us. A peak at or below FreeStackCap with
// leakedRuns 0 is the healthy reading. (Concurrent overflowing pushes in one
// 64-thread free pass can transiently overshoot the true demand by up to the
// wave width; a diagnostic, not a gate, and biased only upward.)
static const uint kCtrOccStackPeak     = 17u;
static const uint kCtrMatStackPeak     = 18u;

// Claim fail reasons, mirrored as FVoxelBrickPool::EGpuClaimFail.
static const uint kFailNone      = 0u;
static const uint kFailWorstCase = 1u;
static const uint kFailOccSpace  = 2u;
static const uint kFailMatSpace  = 3u;
static const uint kFailCollision = 4u;
// Stack-claim split gate: sum(per-chunk totals) != region totals. Counted into
// kCtrClaimFailWorst -- the same verdict ("these totals cannot be trusted; the
// leg is invalid"), distinguishable post-mortem by this reason code. 5, NOT 4:
// it was first written as 4 and collided with kFailCollision above -- two
// failures indistinguishable in the one dword that exists to tell them apart.
static const uint kFailStackSplit = 5u;

static const uint kBrickOffsetMask = 0x0fffffffu;
static const uint kBrickFieldMask  = 0xf0000000u;
static const uint kBrickKindMixed  = 2u;
static const uint kBrickOccWords   = 16u;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// helpers -- each takes the arena's layout as arguments so one body serves both.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Pop a class-sized range. Returns REGION-RELATIVE base via OutRel; false = empty.
bool StackPop(uint TopsFirst, uint StackFirst, uint Class, out uint OutRel)
{
	OutRel = 0u;
	uint Old;
	// 0xffffffffu is two's-complement -1: InterlockedAdd has no signed form on a
	// uint buffer, and the wrap-on-empty case is handled by the restore below.
	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[TopsFirst + Class - 1u], 0xffffffffu, Old);
	if (Old == 0u || Old > FreeStackCap)
	{
		// Empty (or a concurrent pop raced us past zero). Restore and report
		// empty; the caller bumps instead. The unsigned wrap on an empty pop is
		// why the restore is unconditional on this branch.
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[TopsFirst + Class - 1u], 1u);
		return false;
	}
	OutRel = AllocState[StackFirst + (Class - 1u) * FreeStackCap + (Old - 1u)];
	return true;
}

// Push a class-sized range back. False = stack full (the range is LEAKED, which
// the caller counts -- a full stack means frees are outrunning reuse by
// FreeStackCap * step dwords in one class, which the window line surfaces).
// PeakCtr is the arena's kCtr*StackPeak: the demanded depth (Old + 1) is
// recorded BEFORE the overflow check, deliberately, so an overflowing burst
// reports how deep it actually wanted to go -- see the counter's comment.
bool StackPush(uint TopsFirst, uint StackFirst, uint Class, uint Rel, uint PeakCtr)
{
	uint Old;
	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[TopsFirst + Class - 1u], 1u, Old);
	InterlockedMax(AllocState[PeakCtr], Old + 1u);
	if (Old >= FreeStackCap)
	{
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[TopsFirst + Class - 1u], 0xffffffffu);
		return false;
	}
	AllocState[StackFirst + (Class - 1u) * FreeStackCap + Old] = Rel;
	return true;
}

// Set the pages of a region-relative range. Returns the number of pages that were
// ALREADY set -- non-zero means a double grant, the failure this bitmap exists for.
uint BitmapSet(uint BitmapFirst, uint Rel, uint Class, uint Step)
{
	const uint FirstPage = Rel / Step;
	uint Collisions = 0u;
	for (uint P = 0u; P < Class; ++P)
	{
		const uint Page = FirstPage + P;
		uint Old;
		InterlockedOr(AllocBitmap[BitmapFirst + (Page >> 5u)], 1u << (Page & 31u), Old);
		if ((Old & (1u << (Page & 31u))) != 0u)
		{
			++Collisions;
		}
	}
	return Collisions;
}

// Clear the pages of a range. Returns pages that were ALREADY CLEAR (a range freed
// twice, or never granted -- the free-side inconsistency).
uint BitmapClear(uint BitmapFirst, uint Rel, uint Class, uint Step)
{
	const uint FirstPage = Rel / Step;
	uint Missing = 0u;
	for (uint P = 0u; P < Class; ++P)
	{
		const uint Page = FirstPage + P;
		uint Old;
		InterlockedAnd(AllocBitmap[BitmapFirst + (Page >> 5u)], ~(1u << (Page & 31u)), Old);
		if ((Old & (1u << (Page & 31u))) == 0u)
		{
			++Missing;
		}
	}
	return Missing;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// poolClaimChunkAt -- the claim proper, FACTORED (P3 stage 6) so the classic
// per-chunk entry point and the worklist Claim stage run the SAME TEXT (the
// called-not-copied rule every converted stage follows). One thread claims one
// chunk's two arena ranges: totals -> class -> pop-or-bump -> bitmap -> side
// table + the 8-dword claim slice at ClaimBase. SrcOccOut/SrcMatOut land in
// the spare pair [6]/[7] -- the word-copy source base, already summed by the
// caller (stack prefix + SrcWords*Base on the classic path; the record's
// pack-arena slice base on the worklist path).
//
// A failed claim writes ok=0 and a reason; the write passes then write
// NOTHING except a zeroed record, so failure renders as an absent chunk --
// never as somebody else's bytes.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
void poolClaimChunkAt(uint ClaimBase, uint TheChunkSlot,
                      uint OccWords, uint MatWords,
                      uint TheOccWorst, uint TheMatWorst,
                      uint SrcOccOut, uint SrcMatOut)
{
	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 1u] = 0u; OutClaim[ClaimBase + 2u] = 0u;
	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 3u] = 0u; OutClaim[ClaimBase + 4u] = 0u;
	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 6u] = SrcOccOut;
	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 7u] = SrcMatOut;

	// The side table entry is written on EVERY path, including failure, so the
	// free pass for this slot always reads a defined entry. Failure writes
	// class 0 twice -- "nothing to free".
	const uint Side = TheChunkSlot * 4u;

	// A total above the scratch worst case did not come from the scan -- the CPU
	// path refuses these at Deliver for the same reason (a copy sized from it
	// would read past the scratch), and this path must not be softer.
	if (OccWords > TheOccWorst || MatWords > TheMatWorst)
	{
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrClaimFailWorst], 1u);
		AllocSide[Side + 0u] = 0u; AllocSide[Side + 1u] = 0u;
		AllocSide[Side + 2u] = 0u; AllocSide[Side + 3u] = 0u;
		OutClaim[ClaimBase + 0u] = 0u; OutClaim[ClaimBase + 5u] = kFailWorstCase;
		return;
	}

	// --- the claim-size demand histogram (always-on) ------------------------
	// One InterlockedAdd per arena per claim, in a single-thread kernel --
	// unmeasurable next to the copy passes. Counts DEMAND (every total the
	// gates above accepted), not grants: on a leg with claimFail 0 the two are
	// identical, and on a leg with failures the histogram still shows the mix
	// that was asked for -- which is the number step tuning needs. Zero-word
	// arenas are deliberately NOT counted (they allocate nothing and pad
	// nothing); their count is recovered CPU-side as demand minus the bucket
	// sum. Bucket = (words-1)/width, so a bucket's upper edge is exactly
	// (b+1)*width and any step that is a multiple of the width projects
	// EXACTLY onto the buckets -- the harvest's projected-padding line depends
	// on that alignment. The min() cannot clip in today's layout (the worst-
	// case gate above already refused anything past the last bucket's edge);
	// it guards a drifted layout, not a live path.
	if (OccWords > 0u)
	{
		const uint OccBucket = min((OccWords - 1u) / OccHistBucketWords, OccHistBuckets - 1u);
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[OccHistFirst + OccBucket], 1u);
	}
	if (MatWords > 0u)
	{
		const uint MatBucket = min((MatWords - 1u) / MatHistBucketWords, MatHistBuckets - 1u);
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[MatHistFirst + MatBucket], 1u);
	}

	const uint OccClass = (OccWords + OccClassStep - 1u) / OccClassStep;  // 0 when 0
	const uint MatClass = (MatWords + MatClassStep - 1u) / MatClassStep;

	// --- occupancy ---------------------------------------------------------
	uint OccRel = 0u;
	bool bOccOk = true;
	bool bOccFromStack = false;
	if (OccClass > 0u)
	{
		bOccFromStack = StackPop(OccTopsFirst, OccStackFirst, OccClass, OccRel);
		if (!bOccFromStack)
		{
			const uint Size = OccClass * OccClassStep;
			uint Old;
			InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrOccBump], Size, Old);
			if (Old + Size > OccRegionWords)
			{
				// Give the reservation back so the high-water stays honest and a
				// later, smaller class can still fit.
				InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrOccBump], 0u - Size);
				bOccOk = false;
			}
			else
			{
				OccRel = Old;
			}
		}
	}
	if (!bOccOk)
	{
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrClaimFailOcc], 1u);
		AllocSide[Side + 0u] = 0u; AllocSide[Side + 1u] = 0u;
		AllocSide[Side + 2u] = 0u; AllocSide[Side + 3u] = 0u;
		OutClaim[ClaimBase + 0u] = 0u; OutClaim[ClaimBase + 5u] = kFailOccSpace;
		return;
	}

	// --- materials ---------------------------------------------------------
	uint MatRel = 0u;
	bool bMatOk = true;
	bool bMatFromStack = false;
	if (MatClass > 0u)
	{
		bMatFromStack = StackPop(MatTopsFirst, MatStackFirst, MatClass, MatRel);
		if (!bMatFromStack)
		{
			const uint Size = MatClass * MatClassStep;
			uint Old;
			InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrMatBump], Size, Old);
			if (Old + Size > MatRegionWords)
			{
				InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrMatBump], 0u - Size);
				bMatOk = false;
			}
			else
			{
				MatRel = Old;
			}
		}
	}
	if (!bMatOk)
	{
		// Give the occupancy range back before failing -- the same
		// give-back-what-succeeded rule AllocateForChunk's retry loop follows.
		// It is class-sized whether it came from the stack or the bump, so the
		// stack is always a legal home for it; a full stack leaks it, counted.
		if (OccClass > 0u && !StackPush(OccTopsFirst, OccStackFirst, OccClass, OccRel, kCtrOccStackPeak))
		{
			InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrFreePushOverflow], 1u);
		}
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrClaimFailMat], 1u);
		AllocSide[Side + 0u] = 0u; AllocSide[Side + 1u] = 0u;
		AllocSide[Side + 2u] = 0u; AllocSide[Side + 3u] = 0u;
		OutClaim[ClaimBase + 0u] = 0u; OutClaim[ClaimBase + 5u] = kFailMatSpace;
		return;
	}

	// --- the double-grant gate ---------------------------------------------
	uint Collisions = 0u;
	if (OccClass > 0u) { Collisions += BitmapSet(OccBitmapFirst, OccRel, OccClass, OccClassStep); }
	if (MatClass > 0u) { Collisions += BitmapSet(MatBitmapFirst, MatRel, MatClass, MatClassStep); }
	if (Collisions != 0u)
	{
		// The allocator granted dwords somebody else holds. DO NOT write there.
		// The ranges are deliberately leaked (returning them would hand the
		// corruption to the next claim) and the collision counter turns the
		// window line into a FAIL. Bits set by this claim on pages that were
		// clear stay set -- part of the same leak, and the lesser evil against
		// clearing a bit the true owner relies on.
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrBitmapCollision], Collisions);
		AllocSide[Side + 0u] = 0u; AllocSide[Side + 1u] = 0u;
		AllocSide[Side + 2u] = 0u; AllocSide[Side + 3u] = 0u;
		OutClaim[ClaimBase + 0u] = 0u; OutClaim[ClaimBase + 5u] = kFailCollision;
		return;
	}

	// --- success -----------------------------------------------------------
	const uint OccBaseAbs = (OccClass > 0u) ? (OccRegionFirst + OccRel) : 0u;
	const uint MatBaseAbs = (MatClass > 0u) ? (MatRegionFirst + MatRel) : 0u;

	AllocSide[Side + 0u] = OccBaseAbs;
	AllocSide[Side + 1u] = OccClass;
	AllocSide[Side + 2u] = MatBaseAbs;
	AllocSide[Side + 3u] = MatClass;

	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrClaims], 1u);
	if (bOccFromStack || bMatFromStack)
	{
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrStackPops], (bOccFromStack ? 1u : 0u) + (bMatFromStack ? 1u : 0u));
	}
	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrOccInFlight], OccClass * OccClassStep);
	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrMatInFlight], MatClass * MatClassStep);
	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrOccPaddedCum], OccClass * OccClassStep);
	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrOccActualCum], OccWords);
	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrMatPaddedCum], MatClass * MatClassStep);
	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrMatActualCum], MatWords);

	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 0u] = 1u;
	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 1u] = OccBaseAbs;
	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 2u] = OccWords;
	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 3u] = MatBaseAbs;
	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 4u] = MatWords;
	OutClaim[ClaimBase + 5u] = kFailNone;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BrickPoolClaimMain -- the classic per-chunk entry point: ONE thread, totals
// read where the scan produced them (classic pair, worklist arena slice, or a
// fused member's own pair with the in-kernel prefix + split gate), then the
// FACTORED claim proper above -- the same text the worklist Claim stage runs.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[numthreads(1, 1, 1)]
void BrickPoolClaimMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	if (tid.x != 0u)
	{
		return;
	}

	// Stack-claim: a fused member reads its own totals pair and computes its
	// scratch prefix; classic (TotalsChunkIndexPlusOne == 0) reads [0..1] with
	// prefix 0, exactly as before. The prefix lands in OutClaim[6]/[7] -- the
	// documented spare pair -- where the copy/desc kernels read it back; on the
	// classic path those stay 0 and every consumer's arithmetic is the
	// identity, which is what keeps the control byte-identical.
	uint OccWords = 0u;
	uint MatWords = 0u;
	uint SrcOccFirst = 0u;
	uint SrcMatFirst = 0u;
	if (TotalsChunkIndexPlusOne == 0u)
	{
		// TotalsReadBase is 0 on every classic dispatch (see its declaration).
		OccWords = InBrickTotals[TotalsReadBase + 0u];
		MatWords = InBrickTotals[TotalsReadBase + 1u];
	}
	else
	{
		const uint C = TotalsChunkIndexPlusOne - 1u;
		OccWords = InBrickTotals[2u + 2u * C];
		MatWords = InBrickTotals[3u + 2u * C];
		for (uint K = 0u; K < C; ++K)
		{
			SrcOccFirst += InBrickTotals[2u + 2u * K];
			SrcMatFirst += InBrickTotals[3u + 2u * K];
		}
	}

	// --- the split gate (stack-claim only; see TotalsNumChunks) -------------
	// One thread, <=128 extra reads per member: cheap against the copy passes
	// it protects. Runs BEFORE poolClaimChunkAt's side-table write takes the
	// failure path, so a failed gate leaves the same "nothing to free" state
	// as every other refusal. The slice writes here mirror the factored
	// function's failure shape ([1..4] zero, spare pair set) so a consumer of
	// a split-failed claim reads the same bytes it always did.
	if (TotalsNumChunks > 0u)
	{
		uint SumOcc = 0u;
		uint SumMat = 0u;
		for (uint K2 = 0u; K2 < TotalsNumChunks; ++K2)
		{
			SumOcc += InBrickTotals[2u + 2u * K2];
			SumMat += InBrickTotals[3u + 2u * K2];
		}
		if (SumOcc != InBrickTotals[0] || SumMat != InBrickTotals[1])
		{
			InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrClaimFailWorst], 1u);
			const uint SideF = ChunkSlot * 4u;
			AllocSide[SideF + 0u] = 0u; AllocSide[SideF + 1u] = 0u;
			AllocSide[SideF + 2u] = 0u; AllocSide[SideF + 3u] = 0u;
			OutClaim[1] = 0u; OutClaim[2] = 0u; OutClaim[3] = 0u; OutClaim[4] = 0u;
			OutClaim[6] = SrcOccFirst + SrcWordsOccBase;
			OutClaim[7] = SrcMatFirst + SrcWordsMatBase;
			OutClaim[0] = 0u; OutClaim[5] = kFailStackSplit;
			return;
		}
	}

	// P3 worklist Pack stage: SrcWordsOccBase/SrcWordsMatBase relocate the
	// word-copy source into the worklist PACK ARENAS (sliceIndex * the
	// per-record worst-case strides) -- 0 on every classic dispatch, and the
	// stack prefix arithmetic above is untouched (fused stacks take no
	// worklist feed, so the two terms never both fire).
	poolClaimChunkAt(0u, ChunkSlot, OccWords, MatWords,
	                 OccWorstWords, MatWorstWords,
	                 SrcOccFirst + SrcWordsOccBase,
	                 SrcMatFirst + SrcWordsMatBase);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BrickPoolAllocWordCopyMain -- one arena's run, base read FROM THE CLAIM rather
// than from a CPU constant, which is the entire difference from BrickWordCopyMain.
//
// DISPATCHED AT THE WORST CASE (MaxWords threads) because the actual count lives
// on the GPU and an indirect dispatch here would be building P3 early for no
// measured reason (finding #1: pass/dispatch overhead is not the ceiling). The
// worst case is 1,024 occ / 8,448 mat threads per chunk -- 16 / 132 groups --
// and the excess threads read two dwords and exit.
//
// WordsClaimIndex selects the arena's claim pair: 0 = occ (claim[1]/[2]),
// 1 = mat (claim[3]/[4]). One kernel, both arenas, the BrickWordCopyMain model.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The claim-slice decode the copy shares with the worklist Write stage (P3
// stage 6): one arena's landed run -- destination base, ACTUAL word count and
// the source first index (the spare pair: 0 on the classic single-chunk path,
// the member's slice start on a fused stack, the record's pack-arena slice
// base on the worklist path). Returns false on a failed claim.
bool poolAllocWordRun(uint ClaimBase, uint Pair,
                      out uint DstBase, out uint Words, out uint SrcFirst)
{
	DstBase = 0u; Words = 0u; SrcFirst = 0u;
	if (InClaim[ClaimBase + 0u] == 0u)
	{
		return false;
	}
	DstBase  = InClaim[ClaimBase + 1u + 2u * Pair];
	Words    = InClaim[ClaimBase + 2u + 2u * Pair];
	SrcFirst = InClaim[ClaimBase + 6u + Pair];
	return true;
}

[numthreads(64, 1, 1)]
void BrickPoolAllocWordCopyMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint i = tid.x;
	uint Base, Words, SrcFirst;
	if (i >= MaxWords || !poolAllocWordRun(0u, WordsClaimIndex, Base, Words, SrcFirst))
	{
		return;
	}
	if (i >= Words)
	{
		return;
	}
	OutWords[Base + i] = InWords[SrcFirst + i];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// poolAllocRebaseDesc -- the descriptor rebase arithmetic, FACTORED (P3 stage
// 6): the classic desc pass, the worklist ClaimRecord pass and the worklist
// claim verify all call this one text. MIXED only -- a uniform brick's zero
// offsets must stay zero, for VoxelBrickPoolWrite.usf's file-header reason.
// OccSrcFirst/MatSrcFirst are the SOURCE-relative rebase terms (the stack
// prefix; 0 when the source offsets are already relative to the slice read).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
uint2 poolAllocRebaseDesc(uint2 D, uint OccBaseClaim, uint MatBaseClaim,
                          uint OccSrcFirst, uint MatSrcFirst)
{
	const uint Kind = (D.x >> 28u) & 3u;
	if (Kind == kBrickKindMixed)
	{
		const uint OccOffset = ((D.x & kBrickOffsetMask) - OccSrcFirst + OccBaseClaim) & kBrickOffsetMask;
		const uint MatOffset = ((D.y & kBrickOffsetMask) - MatSrcFirst + MatBaseClaim) & kBrickOffsetMask;
		D.x = OccOffset | (D.x & kBrickFieldMask);
		D.y = MatOffset | (D.y & kBrickFieldMask);
	}
	return D;
}

// The desc write proper, shared by the classic entry point and the worklist
// ClaimRecord kernel: fail-zero (a reused descriptor block must not keep its
// previous tenant's descriptors) or rebase-and-write. RebaseSubOcc/Mat are
// subtracted from the claim's spare pair to recover the true rebase term --
// see the audit note in BrickPoolAllocDescMain below.
void poolAllocDescWriteAt(uint ClaimBase, uint SrcIdx, uint DstIdx,
                          uint RebaseSubOcc, uint RebaseSubMat)
{
	if (InClaim[ClaimBase + 0u] == 0u)
	{
		OutBrickDesc[DstIdx] = uint2(0u, 0u);
		return;
	}
	OutBrickDesc[DstIdx] = poolAllocRebaseDesc(
		InBrickDesc[SrcIdx],
		InClaim[ClaimBase + 1u], InClaim[ClaimBase + 3u],
		InClaim[ClaimBase + 6u] - RebaseSubOcc,
		InClaim[ClaimBase + 7u] - RebaseSubMat);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BrickPoolAllocDescMain -- BrickDescPoolWriteMain with the two bases read from
// the claim. The rebase arithmetic is VERBATIM (factored above); only where
// the bases come from changed, and this file's header is the record of why.
//
// Stack-claim: scratch descriptors of a fused member are BATCH-relative
// (the pack is chunk-major over one shared scratch), so the member's slice
// prefix -- the claim's spare pair -- comes back OFF before the pool base
// goes ON. Classic path: SrcDescBase 0, spare pair 0, arithmetic verbatim.
//
// THE SPARE PAIR IS NOT THE REBASE TERM (2026-08-23 stage-6 audit). The
// claim folds SrcWordsOccBase/SrcWordsMatBase -- the worklist Pack
// stage's arena slice bases -- into [6]/[7] so the COPY kernels source
// from the pack arenas. But pack-arena descriptors are CHUNK-relative
// (the stage's one deliberate split), so their rebase term is ZERO, not
// the slice base: subtracting the raw spare pair mis-rebased every
// pack-fed chunk with slice > 0 into garbage pool offsets, invisibly to
// every arena-level gate (the pool desc was never compared). Subtracting
// the host-known SrcWords*Base back out leaves exactly the stack prefix:
// stack (prefix, bases 0), classic (0, 0), pack-fed (0 = base - base).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[numthreads(64, 1, 1)]
void BrickPoolAllocDescMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint i = tid.x;
	if (i >= BrickCount)
	{
		return;
	}
	poolAllocDescWriteAt(0u, SrcDescBase + i, BrickBase + i,
	                     SrcWordsOccBase, SrcWordsMatBase);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The chunk-record composition, FACTORED (P3 stage 6) so the classic record
// pass, the worklist ClaimRecord kernel and the worklist claim verify all run
// the same text.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Per-brick allSolid: kind SOLID is all-solid by definition; kind MIXED reads
// its 16 occupancy dwords from the SOURCE the descriptors were packed against.
// OccSrcWordsBase (2026-08-23 stage-6 audit): the descriptor's occ offset is
// relative to that source -- the shared scratch on the classic/stack paths
// (base 0, offsets batch-relative) but the record's CHUNK-RELATIVE slice on
// the worklist pack path, where InBrickOcc is the whole pack arena. Without
// the base every pack-fed chunk with slice > 0 computed allSolid from slice
// 0's words.
bool poolAllocBrickAllSolid(uint2 D, uint OccSrcWordsBase)
{
	const uint Kind = (D.x >> 28u) & 3u;
	if (Kind == 1u)
	{
		return true;
	}
	if (Kind != kBrickKindMixed)
	{
		return false;
	}
	const uint OccWord = OccSrcWordsBase + (D.x & kBrickOffsetMask);
	uint Acc = 0xffffffffu;
	for (uint k = 0u; k < kBrickOccWords; ++k)
	{
		Acc &= InBrickOcc[OccWord + k];
	}
	return (Acc == 0xffffffffu);
}

uint poolAllocLevelAndFlags(uint TheRingLevel, uint MaskLo, uint MaskHi, uint AllSolid)
{
	const uint AnySolid = ((MaskLo | MaskHi) != 0u) ? 1u : 0u;
	return (TheRingLevel & 0xfu) | (AnySolid << 4u) | (AllSolid << 5u);
}

// Dword d of the 64 B chunk record (0 for the reserved tail). The one list of
// what a record IS; the verify recomputes expectations through this same text.
uint poolAllocRecordDword(uint d, int3 Origin, uint LevelAndFlags, uint TheBrickBase,
                          uint MaskLo, uint MaskHi, uint Climate, uint Grad, uint SurfZ)
{
	if (d == 0u) { return asuint(Origin.x); }
	if (d == 1u) { return asuint(Origin.y); }
	if (d == 2u) { return asuint(Origin.z); }
	if (d == 3u) { return LevelAndFlags; }
	if (d == 4u) { return TheBrickBase; }
	if (d == 5u) { return MaskLo; }
	if (d == 6u) { return MaskHi; }
	if (d == 7u) { return Climate; }
	if (d == 8u) { return Grad; }
	if (d == 9u) { return SurfZ; }
	return 0u;
}

groupshared uint gsAllocAllSolid;

// The cooperative record write: 64 threads reduce allSolid over the bricks,
// thread 0 writes (or fail-zeroes) the record. Callable from any 64-thread
// group whose per-record inputs are the arguments -- the classic entry point
// passes its uniforms, the worklist kernel its record fields.
void poolAllocRecordWriteAt(uint ClaimBase, uint GTidX, uint BrickCountL,
                            uint DescSrcBase, uint OccSrcWordsBase, uint MaskElemBase,
                            uint TheChunkSlot, int3 Origin, uint TheRingLevel,
                            uint TheBrickBase, uint Climate, uint Grad, uint SurfZ)
{
	if (GTidX == 0u)
	{
		gsAllocAllSolid = 1u;
	}
	GroupMemoryBarrierWithGroupSync();

	const bool bOk = (InClaim[ClaimBase + 0u] != 0u);

	if (bOk && GTidX < BrickCountL)
	{
		// Stack-claim: this member's descriptor slice. The occ offsets inside
		// the descriptors stay source-relative here on purpose -- they index
		// what this kernel reads (InBrickOcc), not the pool.
		if (!poolAllocBrickAllSolid(InBrickDesc[DescSrcBase + GTidX], OccSrcWordsBase))
		{
			InterlockedAnd(gsAllocAllSolid, 0u);
		}
	}
	GroupMemoryBarrierWithGroupSync();

	if (GTidX != 0u)
	{
		return;
	}

	const uint Base = TheChunkSlot * ChunkRecordDwords;
	if (!bOk)
	{
		// A FAILED claim zeroes the whole record, so the chunk reads as
		// "nothing here" instead of resurrecting whatever a previous tenant
		// left.
		for (uint Z = 0u; Z < ChunkRecordDwords; ++Z)
		{
			OutChunkTable[Base + Z] = 0u;
		}
		return;
	}

	// Stack-claim: 2 dwords per chunk, chunk-major -- this member's slot
	// (an ELEMENT base). Classic path: 0, same two dwords as always.
	const uint MaskLo = InChunkBrickMask[MaskElemBase + 0u];
	const uint MaskHi = InChunkBrickMask[MaskElemBase + 1u];
	const uint AllSolid = (gsAllocAllSolid != 0u) ? 1u : 0u;
	const uint LevelAndFlags = poolAllocLevelAndFlags(TheRingLevel, MaskLo, MaskHi, AllSolid);

	for (uint D2 = 0u; D2 < ChunkRecordDwords; ++D2)
	{
		OutChunkTable[Base + D2] = poolAllocRecordDword(
			D2, Origin, LevelAndFlags, TheBrickBase, MaskLo, MaskHi, Climate, Grad, SurfZ);
	}
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BrickPoolAllocRecordMain -- BrickChunkRecordMain's body (the allSolid reduce
// is unchanged and still runs over the SCRATCH cell data, for that kernel's
// stated reason), factored above so the worklist Claim stage runs the same
// text. The record format is UNTOUCHED -- the arena ranges live in the side
// table, not in the reserved tail, precisely so every existing record
// consumer and verifier keeps working unmodified.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[numthreads(64, 1, 1)]
void BrickPoolAllocRecordMain(uint3 gtid : SV_GroupThreadID)
{
	poolAllocRecordWriteAt(0u, gtid.x, BrickCount,
	                       SrcDescBase, SrcWordsOccBase, ChunkMaskBase,
	                       ChunkSlot, OriginVoxel, RingLevel,
	                       BrickBase, ChunkClimatePacked, ChunkSurfaceGradPacked,
	                       ChunkSurfaceZRelBits);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BrickPoolFreeMain -- eviction WITHOUT a round trip: the CPU sends the slot
// numbers it decided to evict (it always knew WHICH); this pass looks the ranges
// up in the side table (the knowledge the readback used to carry), pushes them
// onto their class stacks, clears their bitmap pages, zeroes the record and the
// side entry. Nothing comes back. A slot whose claim failed or never ran has a
// zero side entry and frees nothing -- which is why the claim writes the side
// table on every path.
//
// ORDERING IS THE CALLER'S LOAD-BEARING JOB: FVoxelBrickPool enqueues every
// pending free BEFORE it enqueues any batch that could re-claim a freed slot, so
// this pass can never read a side entry the re-claim has already overwritten.
// See FlushPendingGpuFrees in VoxelBrickPool.cpp for the interleave that forced
// that rule.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[numthreads(64, 1, 1)]
void BrickPoolFreeMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint s = tid.x;
	if (s >= NumSlots)
	{
		return;
	}
	const uint Slot = InSlotList[s];
	const uint Side = Slot * 4u;

	// TAKE THE SLOT, DO NOT READ IT. Two threads of this dispatch can be handed
	// the SAME Slot: the CPU's free queue used to append a slot number per
	// FreeResident call with no dedupe, and a same-key re-request inside one
	// batch frees the resident before reallocating -- over an always-coalesced
	// first-fit descriptor arena the realloc hands the SAME slot back, so the
	// two queue entries are one slot twice, not two slots.
	//
	// The old plain reads let both threads see class > 0, so both BitmapCleared
	// the same pages (the loser counting every page as Missing -> badFree) and
	// both StackPushed the same Rel, putting one range on the free stack twice
	// -> a later claim's BitmapSet collided -> doubleGrant. That is the
	// structural doubleGrant == badFree signature on the window line: one pair
	// of threads over one range produces exactly one of each per page.
	//
	// The exchange makes the class words the claim ticket. Exactly one thread
	// can observe a non-zero class for a slot; the duplicate observes 0 and
	// frees nothing, which is the same path a slot whose claim failed has
	// always taken. FlushPendingGpuFrees now dedupes CPU-side and counts the
	// collapses (dupFreeSlots) -- this is the guard a future caller who
	// re-introduces a duplicate cannot bypass, and it needs no new uniform and
	// no new binding: AllocSide is already a UAV on this pass.
	//
	// ORDER MATTERS: the classes are taken BEFORE the bases are read, and the
	// BASE words are zeroed only on the branch that WON its class. A losing
	// thread therefore never writes Side+0 / Side+2, so the winner's read of the
	// base after its exchange cannot race a duplicate's cleanup. (No behaviour
	// changes for a genuine class-0 entry: every writer that stores class 0 also
	// stores base 0, so the removed unconditional zeroing wrote 0 over 0.)
	uint OccClass = 0u;
	uint MatClass = 0u;
	InterlockedExchange(AllocSide[Side + 1u], 0u, OccClass);
	InterlockedExchange(AllocSide[Side + 3u], 0u, MatClass);

	const uint OccBaseAbs = AllocSide[Side + 0u];
	const uint MatBaseAbs = AllocSide[Side + 2u];

	uint Missing = 0u;
	if (OccClass > 0u)
	{
		const uint Rel = OccBaseAbs - OccRegionFirst;
		Missing += BitmapClear(OccBitmapFirst, Rel, OccClass, OccClassStep);
		if (!StackPush(OccTopsFirst, OccStackFirst, OccClass, Rel, kCtrOccStackPeak))
		{
			InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrFreePushOverflow], 1u);
		}
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrOccInFlight], 0u - OccClass * OccClassStep);
		AllocSide[Side + 0u] = 0u;
	}
	if (MatClass > 0u)
	{
		const uint Rel = MatBaseAbs - MatRegionFirst;
		Missing += BitmapClear(MatBitmapFirst, Rel, MatClass, MatClassStep);
		if (!StackPush(MatTopsFirst, MatStackFirst, MatClass, Rel, kCtrMatStackPeak))
		{
			InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrFreePushOverflow], 1u);
		}
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrMatInFlight], 0u - MatClass * MatClassStep);
		AllocSide[Side + 2u] = 0u;
	}
	if (Missing != 0u)
	{
		// A page this range should have owned was already clear: the range was
		// freed twice, or granted past the bitmap. Either is the allocator lying
		// about ownership -- FAIL-grade, surfaced on the window line.
		InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrFreeBitmapMissing], Missing);
	}

	// The class words were already zeroed by the exchanges above and each base
	// word is zeroed by the branch that won it, so the side entry is fully clear
	// here on every path -- including a duplicate's, whose entry the winner
	// clears.
	//
	// The record zeroing and the frees tally stay UNCONDITIONAL, exactly as
	// before: a duplicate thread writes the same zeros the winner writes (no
	// hazard, one value), and `frees` has always counted slot list entries
	// processed, not ranges reclaimed. Changing either would move a number the
	// window line's reconciliation reads.
	for (uint D = 0u; D < ChunkRecordDwords; ++D)
	{
		OutChunkTable[Slot * ChunkRecordDwords + D] = 0u;
	}
	InterlockedAdd(AllocState[kCtrFrees], 1u);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BrickPoolAllocVerifyMain -- the sampled live cross-check, run every stats
// window over a rotor of GPU-allocated resident chunks.
//
// EXPECTATIONS COME FROM THE CPU'S RESIDENT MAP, not from anything this
// allocator wrote -- the derived-not-verified rule. Per entry (8 dwords):
// {slot, originX, originY, originZ, ringLevel, brickBase, 0, 0}.
//
// WHAT FAILS IT (OutVerify[0]):
//   * a NON-ZERO record whose origin, ring level or BrickBase disagree with what
//     the CPU allocated the slot for -- one chunk's record describing another's
//     ground, the exact silent corruption this phase must not ship;
//   * a side-table range outside its GPU region or misaligned to its class step;
//   * a side-table range whose bitmap pages are NOT all set -- ranges a free
//     already reclaimed while the chunk is still resident.
//
// An ALL-ZERO record (dwords 0..6) counts into OutVerify[2] instead: the write
// has not landed, the claim failed, or the job was abandoned -- diagnosed
// CPU-side against the claim-fail counters, and NOT a corruption verdict. (A
// real all-air chunk keyed exactly at origin (0,0,0) L0 with BrickBase 0 also
// reads as this; one chunk in the world, an undercount and never a false FAIL.)
// OutVerify[1] counts chunks checked, so a verify that checked nothing reads as
// 0/0 -- "no verdict", never "pass". The vxc_gpu rule.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[numthreads(64, 1, 1)]
void BrickPoolAllocVerifyMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint e = tid.x;
	if (e >= NumEntries)
	{
		return;
	}
	const uint Base = e * 8u;
	const uint Slot      = InExpect[Base + 0u];
	const uint ExpOx     = InExpect[Base + 1u];
	const uint ExpOy     = InExpect[Base + 2u];
	const uint ExpOz     = InExpect[Base + 3u];
	const uint ExpLevel  = InExpect[Base + 4u];
	const uint ExpBrickBase = InExpect[Base + 5u];

	const uint Rec = Slot * ChunkRecordDwords;
	bool bAllZero = true;
	for (uint D = 0u; D < 7u; ++D)
	{
		bAllZero = bAllZero && (InPoolTable[Rec + D] == 0u);
	}
	if (bAllZero)
	{
		InterlockedAdd(OutVerify[2], 1u);
		InterlockedAdd(OutVerify[1], 1u);
		return;
	}

	uint Mismatches = 0u;
	Mismatches += (InPoolTable[Rec + 0u] != ExpOx) ? 1u : 0u;
	Mismatches += (InPoolTable[Rec + 1u] != ExpOy) ? 1u : 0u;
	Mismatches += (InPoolTable[Rec + 2u] != ExpOz) ? 1u : 0u;
	Mismatches += ((InPoolTable[Rec + 3u] & 0xfu) != (ExpLevel & 0xfu)) ? 1u : 0u;
	Mismatches += (InPoolTable[Rec + 4u] != ExpBrickBase) ? 1u : 0u;

	// The side table's ranges: in-region, class-aligned, and still owned per the
	// bitmap. Read-only bindings of the same buffers the allocator writes.
	const uint Side = Slot * 4u;
	const uint OccBaseAbs = InAllocSide[Side + 0u];
	const uint OccClass   = InAllocSide[Side + 1u];
	const uint MatBaseAbs = InAllocSide[Side + 2u];
	const uint MatClass   = InAllocSide[Side + 3u];

	if (OccClass > 0u)
	{
		const uint Rel = OccBaseAbs - OccRegionFirst;
		const bool bInRegion = (OccBaseAbs >= OccRegionFirst)
			&& (Rel + OccClass * OccClassStep <= OccRegionWords)
			&& ((Rel % OccClassStep) == 0u);
		if (!bInRegion)
		{
			++Mismatches;
		}
		else
		{
			const uint FirstPage = Rel / OccClassStep;
			for (uint P = 0u; P < OccClass; ++P)
			{
				const uint Page = FirstPage + P;
				if ((InAllocBitmap[OccBitmapFirst + (Page >> 5u)] & (1u << (Page & 31u))) == 0u)
				{
					++Mismatches;
				}
			}
		}
	}
	if (MatClass > 0u)
	{
		const uint Rel = MatBaseAbs - MatRegionFirst;
		const bool bInRegion = (MatBaseAbs >= MatRegionFirst)
			&& (Rel + MatClass * MatClassStep <= MatRegionWords)
			&& ((Rel % MatClassStep) == 0u);
		if (!bInRegion)
		{
			++Mismatches;
		}
		else
		{
			const uint FirstPage = Rel / MatClassStep;
			for (uint P = 0u; P < MatClass; ++P)
			{
				const uint Page = FirstPage + P;
				if ((InAllocBitmap[MatBitmapFirst + (Page >> 5u)] & (1u << (Page & 31u))) == 0u)
				{
					++Mismatches;
				}
			}
		}
	}

	if (Mismatches != 0u)
	{
		InterlockedAdd(OutVerify[0], Mismatches);
	}
	InterlockedAdd(OutVerify[1], 1u);
}
